          "question": "Why does distilled water not conduct electricity, whereas rain water does?",
          "solution": [
            "<b>Scientific Reason:</b>",
            "1. <b>Conduction in Liquids Requires Free Ions:</b> For any liquid to conduct an electric current, it must contain free, mobile electrical charge carriers (ions).",
            "2. <b>Distilled Water:</b> Distilled water is pure demineralized water. It consists almost entirely of neutral H<sub>2</sub>O molecules with an extremely minute degree of self-ionization ([H<sup>+</sup>] = [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> M), which is insufficient to conduct electricity.",
            "3. <b>Rain Water:</b> As raindrops fall through the atmosphere, they dissolve atmospheric gases such as carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>), sulphur dioxide (SO<sub>2</sub>), and nitrogen oxides (NO<sub>2</sub>).",
            "4. These dissolved gases react with water to form weak and strong mineral acids (carbonic acid H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>, sulphurous acid H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub>, nitric acid HNO<sub>3</sub>):",
            "CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>(aq) &rarr; 2H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq)",
            "5. These dissolved acids dissociate into abundant free hydrogen and acid radical ions, making rain water an <b>effective electrical conductor</b>."
          ],
          "answer": "Distilled water is pure and contains no dissolved ions to conduct current. Rain water dissolves atmospheric gases like CO₂ to form acids (H₂CO₃), providing free ions that conduct electricity."
